   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( y = f(x) \\) be the particular solution to the differential equation \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = 3\\left(1 + y^{2}\\right) \\) with \\( f(0) = -1 \\). The solution exists on a largest open interval \\( (a, b) \\) containing \\( x = 0 \\). Find \\( b \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Divide",
         "Pi",
         4
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Separate and antidifferentiate: \\( \\arctan y = 3 x + C \\), so \\( y = \\tan(3 x + C) \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            3,
            "x"
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At \\( x = 0 \\): \\( C = \\arctan(-1) - 0 = - \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            [
               "Divide",
               "Pi",
               4
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The tangent is undefined where \\( 3 x + C = \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\), which gives the neighbours \\( x = - \\frac{\\pi}{12} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\) of \\( x = 0 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            [
               "Divide",
               "Pi",
               12
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The interval containing \\( x = 0 \\) is \\( \\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{12}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\), so \\( b = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            "Pi",
            4
         ]
      }
